WEBVTT
NOTE OpenIE Research TV v3.0 — ai-coding-market (180s) Narrator Osato

1
00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:07.491
First, headline trillion-dollar figures for AI coding are future guesses, not counts of money already earned.

2
00:00:08.051 --> 00:00:14.878
Second, almost every forecast assumes developers will pay a giant AI model by the word forever.

3
00:00:15.438 --> 00:00:21.291
Third, saving and reusing working code drives repeated jobs toward electricity cost,

4
00:00:21.291 --> 00:00:23.520
making those forecasts too high.

5
00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:31.716
Venture and consulting slides arrive at trillions by multiplying roughly thirty million professional developers—or six times that

6
00:00:31.716 --> 00:00:40.653
many open-source accounts—by assumed hundred-thousand-dollar output gains across the world economy. Meanwhile, actual buyer surveys put 2025 enterprise

7
00:00:40.653 --> 00:00:48.347
coding-tool spend at four billion dollars, and leading coding assistants report annualized run rates in the single-digit billions.

8
00:00:48.907 --> 00:00:55.861
At published list rates, one agentic coding query with a hundred-thousand-word context and ten thousand output words

9
00:00:55.861 --> 00:01:02.695
costs two dollars and twenty-five cents—nearly ten thousand dollars a year per developer at three queries an hour.

10
00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:09.649
Companies blow through annual budgets by spring because every recurring migration or bug fix is billed from scratch.

11
00:01:10.209 --> 00:01:15.916
To stop blending forecasts with bank statements, our study tags every single claim with one

12
00:01:15.916 --> 00:01:24.068
of five strict labels: assumption-driven projections, company-reported run rates and valuations, buyer surveys, audited regulatory

13
00:01:24.068 --> 00:01:31.468
filings such as multi-billion-dollar quarterly equity losses, and reproducible measured runs whose artifacts are kept.

14
00:01:32.028 --> 00:01:38.690
Even without reuse, fierce competition and open weights have driven the price of reaching a fixed coding

15
00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:45.287
benchmark down roughly ten to thirteen times per year since 2023. Verified reuse adds a second, steeper

16
00:01:45.287 --> 00:01:52.205
drop: once a checked fix becomes a deterministic local routine, every repeat arrival needs zero model calls.

17
00:01:52.765 --> 00:01:59.628
In our three-dimensional global compute model, large cloud models remain genuinely valuable when exploring unfamiliar

18
00:01:59.628 --> 00:02:06.255
specifications for the first time, while recurring code transformations crystallize into tiny checked programs on

19
00:02:06.255 --> 00:02:12.355
local machines. Economic value shifts away from metered word volume and toward verified problem closure.

20
00:02:12.915 --> 00:02:20.571
Compilers, relational databases, and open operating systems created trillions of dollars in global economic surplus without

21
00:02:20.571 --> 00:02:27.418
charging programmers a toll every time code compiled. Measured studies across eight coding models already show

22
00:02:27.418 --> 00:02:34.451
agent loops burning a thousand times more words than chat, varying thirtyfold per run without improving accuracy.

23
00:02:35.011 --> 00:02:41.814
Our essay makes no stock-market predictions and claims no external hardware power meter. Instead, it sets out

24
00:02:41.814 --> 00:02:49.990
clean, refutable propositions on public numbers: if verified software reuse works as engineering expects, model calls, dollars, and

25
00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:57.666
joules per merged repository change will fall steadily toward local electricity cost even as world software output expands.
